WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.000
From low Earth orbit to deep space, NASA leads the way in exploration, science, and planetary defense.

00:06.000 --> 00:09.000
Here's what's new this week in Your NASA Minute.

00:09.000 --> 00:13.000
At Kennedy Space Center, teams are continuing to prepare for the Artemis II mission.

00:13.000 --> 00:20.000
This week, the closeout crew practiced sealing Orion's hatch and managers conducted a flight-rendiness review.

00:20.000 --> 00:25.000
With maintenance nearly complete, teams remained focused on a potential launch in early April.

00:25.000 --> 00:28.000
Stay tuned for more updates on America's return to the Moon.

00:28.000 --> 00:32.000
The International Space Station has been busy with cargo operations.

00:32.000 --> 00:38.000
Over the last two weeks, flight controllers used the station's robotic arm to release two resupply spacecraft —

00:38.000 --> 00:42.000
Northrop Grumman's Cygnus XL and Japan's HTV-X1.

00:42.000 --> 00:49.000
Cygnus is headed for re-entry and disposal, while HTV-X1 will stay in orbit to support scientific research.

00:49.000 --> 00:57.000
And finally, a new study of NASA's DART mission confirmed that for the first time, humanity has changed the motion of a celestial body.

00:58.000 --> 01:02.000
The spacecraft struck the asteroid Moonlit DeMorphos in 2022,

01:02.000 --> 01:07.000
shifting its orbit and slightly changing the path of the larger asteroid, Dedimos.

01:07.000 --> 01:12.000
This shows how a spacecraft could one day deflect a potentially hazardous asteroid away from Earth,

01:12.000 --> 01:18.000
and NASA's Planetary Defense Coordination Office keeps watch for those objects long before they become a threat.

01:18.000 --> 01:21.000
Thanks for watching. We'll see you next time on the NASA Minute.

01:27.000 --> 01:30.000
you

